{
  docIndexTitle: "MyJarvis guide",
  docIndexB1: "This guide covers day-to-day use: how to ask, what it handles, and which actions it stops to confirm. Twenty minutes end to end; in a hurry, start with install and the first errand.",
  docIndexB2: "Each page covers one subject and states its limits: what works, what does not, and when it comes back to you.",
  docIndexB3: "If a page disagrees with the build on your desktop, trust the build; the page will be updated.",

  docSetupTitle: "Install and sign in",
  docSetupB1: "Installation takes a couple of minutes. Sign in once and it waits in the system tray.",
  docSetupB2: "Pick your platform on the download page, double-click the installer and accept the defaults. Windows 10 or later 64-bit; macOS 13 or later with Intel and Apple silicon builds.",
  docSetupB3: "If the system blocks an unidentified developer, allow it once under Privacy & Security.",
  docSetupB4: "The first launch asks you to sign in with a phone number and a code. This binds your data and devices to the account so a new computer can pick up.",
  docSetupB5: "It then asks three things: your main mailbox (used when writing as you), where your calendar lives (booking and free time), and where your files are (finding and sorting). Skip anything you are unsure about and add it later.",
  docSetupB6: "A tray icon shows the state: Working means normal, Paused means click to resume. To remove it, uninstall the app and delete the data folder; uninstalling keeps your data so a reinstall continues.",

  docStartTitle: "One first errand",
  docStartB1: "Do not start with an important email. Sorting files, drafting text or turning meeting notes into a to-do list are easy to correct.",
  docStartB2: "State the person, the time and the outcome the way you would to a colleague: “Email the contract on my desktop to Zhang and book an hour on Thursday afternoon.”",
  docStartB3: "It repeats the task back, such as “Zhang, Thursday 14:00 to 15:00, correct?” It starts only after you agree.",
  docStartB4: "Each step appears in the thread. Sending, deleting and paying stop and wait for you.",
  docStartB5: "Say “make it more formal”, “move it to Friday” or “swap the attachment” and it adjusts in place.",
  docStartB6: "Three traps: too much at once, vague timing, and leaving it to decide. Split the errand, name the deadline, and state the outcome you want.",

  docUsesTitle: "What it handles",
  docUsesB1: "Messages and email, calendar and reminders, files and folders, tables and summaries. Each has its limits and each asks before irreversible steps.",
  docUsesB2: "What it will not do: send or pay without consent, upload your material to the cloud, or make decisions that need professional judgement.",
  docUsesB3: "Where it is weaker: live web data and repeated third-party sign-ins. Confirm the last step yourself there.",

  docMessagesTitle: "Messages and email",
  docMessagesB1: "The most used area. It reads context and writes in your voice, but shows you everything before sending.",
  docMessagesB2: "It condenses long threads, drafts in your voice, turns group conclusions into one message, and picks the unread items that need you.",
  docMessagesB3: "Three things are enough: who, what, and tone. Without a tone it follows your earlier messages.",
  docMessagesB4: "Recipients it cannot confirm are asked about; money or commitments get a draft for review; tense exchanges get a warning; attachments are listed for confirmation.",
  docMessagesB5: "It never deletes mail. Ask it to list candidates and choose yourself.",

  docCalendarTitle: "Calendar and reminders",
  docCalendarB1: "It books around your free time, moves meetings, adds reminders, and offers alternatives on conflicts.",
  docCalendarB2: "It extracts time, duration and attendees, shows free blocks, moves ad-hoc meetings, and adds reminders whose lead time you can change.",
  docCalendarB3: "Limits: meetings with three or more people need your confirmation; time zones are converted and read back; cancellations are suggested, not performed.",
  docCalendarB4: "With more than one calendar, say which to use. It asks rather than picking a default.",

  docFilesTitle: "Files and folders",
  docFilesB1: "It finds, renames, files and de-duplicates inside your folders. Irreversible actions are listed for approval first.",
  docFilesB2: "It finds by content, renames by a rule, gathers downloads and desktop files, and lists duplicates for you to decide.",
  docFilesB3: "Deleting lists first, overwriting warns about older versions, system folders are questioned, and bulk renames show the first few results.",
  docFilesB4: "It works only in folders you allow. Widen the scope explicitly when needed.",

  docTablesTitle: "Tables and summaries",
  docTablesB1: "Turns scattered records into a table and long documents into a page. Its most reliable area.",
  docTablesB2: "It collects expenses from threads and mail, turns notes into tasks with owners, reduces long reports to one page, and fills missing columns.",
  docTablesB3: "Every number carries a source; cells without evidence stay blank and flagged; totals state how they were calculated.",
  docTablesB4: "Suitable for organising, summarising and extracting. Not suitable for audited financial statements or final filings.",

  docPrivacyTitle: "Privacy and safety",
  docPrivacyB1: "Your material stays on your computer by default. Conversations and preferences live in the local data folder; the account service keeps sign-in data only.",
  docPrivacyB2: "Network is used to send mail and messages (you see the content first), to read and sync your calendar, and for steps that need model inference, which send only what that step requires and are marked in the thread.",
  docPrivacyB3: "Always confirmed by you: sending, deleting or overwriting, paying or committing, and widening folder access.",
  docPrivacyB4: "Settings → Privacy shows the last network activity and can pause the network while keeping local abilities. Cleanup has three levels: conversations, preferences, and the whole data folder.",

  docBillingTitle: "Membership and billing",
  docBillingB1: "Prices and quotas are shown for your account rather than printed on the portal.",
  docBillingB2: "Entry covers trying it out with base operations. Personal adds longer runs, more operations and several mailboxes. Team adds shared material, roles, audit trail and agreed support.",
  docBillingB3: "Local work incurs no external cost, network steps count against your quota, and prices appear in your account page including regional differences.",
  docBillingB4: "When the quota runs out, local abilities keep working and network steps pause with a prompt. Cancelling returns you to Entry; usage already incurred is still billed.",

  docDevicesTitle: "Devices and sync",
  docDevicesB1: "Each computer runs its own assistant; the account links them.",
  docDevicesB2: "Synced: account and sign-in state, tier and quota, and preferences you mark as portable. Not synced: conversations, local file lists and results, and folder permissions per device.",
  docDevicesB3: "The split keeps a home computer and a work computer separate. To share material, place the data folder on a synced drive but avoid writing from both machines at once.",
  docDevicesB4: "For a new computer: install, sign in with the same account, re-authorise mail, calendar and folders, then sign out on the old machine. The account page lists devices and last activity, and unknown devices can be removed.",

  docTroubleshootingTitle: "Troubleshooting",
  docTroubleshootingB1: "No response: check whether the tray says Paused, or the window shows a waiting-for-confirmation state. Otherwise restart; conversations are kept.",
  docTroubleshootingB2: "Cannot send mail: confirm the mailbox authorisation, the connection, and whether it is waiting on a recipient. Offline sends queue and ask before resending.",
  docTroubleshootingB3: "Cannot find a file: check the folder is allowed, and add the time and source, such as “the one Zhang sent in March”.",
  docTroubleshootingB4: "Wrong result: say precisely what is wrong. To stop a habit, say “stop filing downloads automatically”; rules can be deleted in settings.",
  docTroubleshootingB5: "If none of this helps, export diagnostics from settings (no file contents) and send them to support.",

  docFaqTitle: "Questions",
  docFaqB1: "How is it different from a chatbot? A chatbot answers; this one acts — it sends the mail, moves the meeting, touches the file, and reports each step.",
  docFaqB2: "Does it read my conversations? Content is processed locally; network steps are marked in the thread. Offline file sorting and drafting still work.",
  docFaqB3: "Can it change things by accident? Deleting, sending and paying always ask first, with a list to review.",
  docFaqB4: "Does it cost money? Prices and quotas come from your account; local work incurs no external cost. There is no phone app yet.",
  docFaqB5: "Can I use my own model or key? Bind it in the account page and those calls use your quota."
}
